# Expectation record for 05_pointers/002_differences_and_comparisons.c
#
# Reproduce one cell by hand with no harness: render the three command templates
# below, substituting the target triple, the optimization level, the source path,
# the output path, and the target's runner (empty on the natively executing target).

program            = 002_differences_and_comparisons
area               = 05_pointers
description        = Pointer differences and relational comparisons, with a one-past-end sentinel never dereferenced
targets            = x86_64, i686, aarch64, riscv64
opt_levels         = -O0, -O1, -O2
shared_flags       = -static
bcc_command        = $BCC --target <triple> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
ref_command        = $REF_CC_<TRIPLE> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
run_command        = <runner> <out>
expect_exit        = 0
oracle_a           = enabled
oracle_b           = enabled
oracle_c           = enabled
ub_notes           <<END
A pointer one past the last element is formed, compared and subtracted but never
dereferenced, which is exactly what the standard permits.  A pointer TWO past the end is
never formed at all: each strided walk advances only while a full stride of elements
genuinely remains, and the overshoot a further stride would have produced is derived by
integer arithmetic from the last legal difference instead.  Every difference is between
pointers into the same array object.  Each result is cast to long long before printing, so
the text does not depend on the width of ptrdiff_t.  No address is printed.
Every property is computed twice, once from literal constants and once from
volatile-qualified storage, so the constant folder's answer cannot stand in for the
one the backend computes at run time.
No header is included and printf is declared by hand, so nothing here depends on a
standard-library header bcc does not ship.
END
